The reported fault — Campus 2 host 10.5.0.1 (pc1) being unable to reach 172.16.30.x (Campus 1 VLAN30) — does not exist in the live lab. Direct end-to-end ping tests from pc1 to all three active 172.16.30.x hosts (pc12 at .12, pc13 at .13, pc14 at .14) succeed with sub-3 ms round-trip times and 0% loss.

A thorough examination of the full data and control plane found nothing wrong. All six backbone routers (router7, router8, router10, router11, router16, router17) carry consistent OSPF routes to 172.16.30.0/24 via the expected path: sshswitch → router17 → router8/router10 → router7 → router16 → mswitch0. mswitch0's vlan30@br0 interface (172.16.30.254/24) is up, switch3 has a correctly configured br_vlan30 bridge with VLAN30-tagged uplink eth0.30@eth0 to mswitch0 and untagged access ports to the PCs. No ACLs, route-maps, or distribute-lists appear on any router. Campus 2 multilayer switch sshswitch has the br_v5 gateway (10.5.0.254) for VLAN5, routes 172.16.30.0/24 via 55.5.1.1 (router17), and pings 172.16.30.12 successfully from its own control plane. VLAN10 (172.16.10.5) and VLAN20 (172.16.20.9) are likewise reachable from pc1.

The network is fully healthy. The help-desk ticket describes a connectivity problem that is not present — this is a false-premise report. No corrective action is required.
